# =============================================================================
# API production settings (TEMPLATE) for Windows Server + IIS (iis-deploy-kit).
#
# The real file lives on the server at <server root>\config\app.env (for example
# C:\MyApp\config\app.env). Only Administrators and SYSTEM can read it.
#
# How it works
#   * setup-server.ps1 copies this template there ONCE (an existing file is never touched)
#     and fills the {{...}} tokens on KEY=VALUE lines. Tokens in comment lines are left
#     alone on purpose, so this header stays readable:
#       {{DB_NAME}} {{DB_USER}} {{DB_PORT}}     from deploy.config.json (database)
#       {{API_HOST}} {{WEB_HOST}}               from deploy.config.json (domains)
#       {{API_ORIGIN}} {{WEB_ORIGIN}}           https://<api host>, https://<web host>
#       {{WEB_ALIAS_ORIGIN_n}}                  https://<webAliases[n]> (n = 0, 1, ...)
#       {{STORAGE_DIR}}                         <server root>\storage (API pool may write)
#       {{API_READABLE_DIR}}                    <server root>\config\api-readable (API pool may read)
#       {{GENERATED_DB_PASSWORD}}               password of the database role (random)
#       {{GENERATED_SECRET}}                    a NEW random 64-character secret per occurrence
#   * The application does NOT read this file. set-config.ps1 validates it and copies every
#     value into the IIS application pool's environment variables, then restarts the pool.
#     Secrets therefore never live in the site folder or in web.config.
#
# After every change (elevated PowerShell on the server):
#   notepad C:\MyApp\config\app.env
#   C:\MyApp\bin\set-config.ps1
#
# Rules
#   * Format: KEY=VALUE. No spaces around "=", no trailing comments (only full-line comments
#     starting with #). Keys may contain letters, digits and underscores only.
#   * A double underscore is the configuration section separator:
#     ConnectionStrings__Default == ConnectionStrings:Default.
#   * A value that still contains <...> or {{...}} is treated as "not filled in" and
#     set-config.ps1 refuses to continue. Fill it in or comment the line out.
#     Uncommented a line that contains {{...}} tokens? Run: set-config.ps1 -FillPlaceholders
#   * ASPNETCORE_ENVIRONMENT=Production is added by set-config.ps1; do not repeat it here.
#     ASPNETCORE_URLS / ports are ignored under IIS (addresses come from the IIS bindings).
#   * This template is committed to your repository: it must NEVER contain a real value.
# =============================================================================

# --- Database -----------------------------------------------------------------
# [REQUIRED][SECRET] Local PostgreSQL, listening on localhost only.
# The key name must match "api.connectionStringKey" in deploy.config.json.
ConnectionStrings__Default=Host=127.0.0.1;Port={{DB_PORT}};Database={{DB_NAME}};Username={{DB_USER}};Password={{GENERATED_DB_PASSWORD}};Maximum Pool Size=50

# --- Hosting ------------------------------------------------------------------
# Accepted Host headers. The deploy scripts probe the API locally with the real host name.
AllowedHosts={{API_HOST}};localhost;127.0.0.1

# --- Browser client (CORS) ----------------------------------------------------
# Exact origins of the web site (scheme + host, no trailing slash).
Cors__AllowedOrigins__0={{WEB_ORIGIN}}
# Cors__AllowedOrigins__1={{WEB_ALIAS_ORIGIN_0}}

# --- File storage (optional) ----------------------------------------------------
# Persistent folder that survives releases and is included in the weekly backup.
# Storage__RootPath={{STORAGE_DIR}}

# --- Example: JWT authentication (optional) ------------------------------------
# Uncomment if your API issues tokens. Then run set-config.ps1 -FillPlaceholders to
# fill the {{...}} tokens (a new random secret for {{GENERATED_SECRET}}). Changing the
# signing key signs everybody out.
# Jwt__Issuer={{API_ORIGIN}}
# Jwt__Audience={{WEB_ORIGIN}}
# Jwt__SigningKey={{GENERATED_SECRET}}

# --- Example: reverse proxy (optional) -----------------------------------------
# IIS in-process hosting terminates TLS in the same process: there is no proxy in front of
# the app, so do NOT trust X-Forwarded-* headers. Only if you later put a load balancer/CDN
# in front of the server, enable your app's forwarded-headers support for that proxy's IP.
# ForwardedHeaders__KnownProxies__0=<proxy-ip>

# --- Example: third-party credentials -------------------------------------------
# [SECRET] Fill in manually. A <...> value blocks set-config.ps1 until it is replaced.
# Smtp__Host=<smtp-host>
# Smtp__Password=<smtp-password>
# Credential FILES (e.g. a service-account JSON) go to {{API_READABLE_DIR}}:
# MyService__CredentialsPath={{API_READABLE_DIR}}\service-account.json
